# Goal Description

Implement two key analytics and user-retention features for the Ofast-X plugin:
1. **Deactivation Feedback Form:** A popup that appears when a user clicks "Deactivate" on the WordPress plugins page, asking them why they are leaving. The feedback will be sent directly to your custom API endpoint on `ofastshop.com`.
2. **Opt-in Telemetry (Active Install Tracker):** An opt-in banner shown upon plugin activation asking free/paid users to share anonymous usage data. This allows you to track exactly how many active installs you have.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **API Infrastructure:** This plan assumes you will build (or already have) endpoints on your main website (`https://ofastshop.com/wp-json/ofast-analytics/v1/`) to receive this data. The plugin will send the data, but your server needs to be ready to catch it!

> [!WARNING]
> **Privacy Compliance:** We must make the telemetry *strictly opt-in* (the user must click "Allow") to comply with WordPress.org repository guidelines and GDPR. 

## Open Questions

1. Do you already have API endpoints ready on `ofastshop.com` to receive this data, or should the plugin just send an email to your support address for now?
2. Are you planning to host the free version of this plugin on the official WordPress.org repository? (If yes, we must be extremely careful with the opt-in rules).

## Proposed Changes

---

### `includes/analytics/` (New Folder)

We will create a new dedicated folder for analytics to keep the core clean.

#### [NEW] `includes/analytics/class-ofast-telemetry.php`
- **Purpose:** Handles the "Opt-in" banner displayed when the plugin is first activated.
- **Logic:**
  - Hook into `admin_notices` to show a dismissible banner: "Help us improve Ofast Toolkit by opting in to anonymous usage tracking."
  - If they click "Allow", generate a unique site ID (`ofast_site_id`), save it, and send a `POST` request to `ofastshop.com` with their PHP version, WP version, and activation status.
  - Add a weekly WP-Cron job to "ping" your server so you know they are still active.

#### [NEW] `includes/analytics/class-ofast-deactivation-survey.php`
- **Purpose:** Injects the popup modal on the `plugins.php` admin page.
- **Logic:**
  - Enqueue a custom JavaScript file (`deactivation.js`) only on the `plugins.php` page.
  - Intercept the click event on the "Deactivate" link for Ofast Toolkit.
  - Show a customized HTML modal with radio buttons (e.g., "I found a bug", "It's too hard to use", "Temporary deactivation").
  - When submitted, send an AJAX request to your `ofastshop.com` API with the reason, then proceed to actually deactivate the plugin.

#### [NEW] `assets/js/ofast-deactivation.js`
- **Purpose:** The JavaScript file that handles the modal UI, the animation, the click interception, and the background AJAX request before allowing the default WordPress deactivation URL to trigger.

#### [NEW] `assets/css/ofast-deactivation.css`
- **Purpose:** Styles for the modal to make it look clean and professional (matching the Ofast brand design).

---

### Core Plugin Setup

#### [MODIFY] `ofast-x.php`
- Include the new analytics classes during `ofast_x_init_plugin()`.
- Instantiate the Telemetry and Deactivation Survey classes so they boot up correctly.

## Verification Plan

### Automated Tests
- None required for this UI/API integration.

### Manual Verification
1. **Telemetry:** Activate the plugin on a fresh site. Verify the opt-in banner appears. Click "Allow" and check the network tab to ensure the payload is sent to `ofastshop.com`.
2. **Deactivation:** Go to the Plugins page. Click "Deactivate" on Ofast Toolkit. Verify the modal pops up and prevents immediate deactivation. Select a reason, click submit, verify the network payload sends, and confirm the plugin successfully deactivates afterward.
